ent and site recovery plus an interpretation, 'extra runs resample the wrong basin'); it describes future measurements/decisions, not an external factual claim.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:test_summary\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"(no page fetched — no source cited)\",\"note\":\"No source cited. Counts (36 pairs, 3 AlphaFold-Multimer s...\n  Line 43: {\"ts\":\"2026-10-21T03:57:59.224Z\",\"type\":\"result\",\"n\":10,\"label\":\"seed:idea:seed:dramaturg-2-resample\",\"key\":\"b6c0ca53737d80c05a2c9eff387f34136b69415c809454f76a6c5d132e640a21\",\"value\":{\"items\":[{\"ref\":\"card:question\",\"status\":\"PARTIAL\",\"page_quote\":\"This structure reveals that of the five Ig-like polycystic kidney disease (PKD) domains in AAVR, PKD2 binds directly to the spike region of the AAV2 capsid adjacent to the icosahedral three-fold axis. | SPR confirms that the engineered capsids AAV9-X1.1 and CAP-Mac gained direct binding interactions with human LRP6.\",\"note\":\"The card cites no sources. The fetched pages corroborate published bind/no-bind readouts for AAVR (Zhang et al. abstract: PKD2 binds the AAV2 spike; Fig. 4 is 'Mutagenesis study of AAVR for AAV2 binding assays') and for LRP6 (Shay et al. Fig. 4b/d: SPR shows engineered capsids bind human LRP6/LRP6-E1E2 while parent AAV9 does not), and the AAVR interface is at the threefold axis. No fetched page mentions ALPL or any published ALPL bind/no-bind readout, so that part of the premise is unsupported.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:hypothesis\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"This structure reveals that of the five Ig-like polycystic kidney disease (PKD) domains in AAVR, PKD2 binds directly to the spike region of the AAV2 capsid adjacent to the icosahedral three-fold axis.\",\"note\":\"Quote appears verbatim in the abstract of the cited page and supports the threefold-axis engagement premise (AAVR-PKD2 at the AAV2 spike adjacent to the icosahedral threefold axis). The hypothesis itself (pose enumeration recovering published bind/no-bind labels) is what the plan will predict and needs no source; the insert-loop and plate-/bead-assay wording is plan framing not carried by this source.\"},{\"ref\":\"card:if_lever\",\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"page_quote\":\"AAV2, EMD-9671 and 6IH9; AAV2–AAVR complex, EMD-9672 and 6IHB.\",\"note\":\"The 0.80/0.55 balanced accuracies, 432-vs-54 run counts and 5 Å criterion are the plan's own predicted pilot outcom...\n\n/home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/blind-01/20261021-0116-loop/out/r1/card/run.json:\n  Line 24:     \"text\": \"Enumerate binding modes of capsid libraries against the human BBB-receptor set used by Capsida's NR001–NR005 platform and Lir's Starfish capsid-structure pipeline (ALPL, AAVR, LRP6 and their panel partners) with docking, MD refinement and AlphaFold-Multimer, emitting calls in the format of plate- and bead-based capsid–receptor binding assays. Score whether 3-fold-axis engagement geometry agrees with measured bind/no-bind receptor by receptor, then rank the binding-positive pairs as the only candidates worth a transcytosis claim. Published modeling covers only single-digit complexes, and neither Capsida's nor Lir's structural pipeline has been assessed against exhaustive enumeration. Narrowing: Leaves aside transcytosis and all post-binding fate — the observable is binding agreement plus interface geometry, not fate. It still moves the root question by narrowing the candidate signature catalog (Q3) to pairs that survive a human-receptor binding assay, which is the precondition for any sufficiency test (Q1).\",\n\n\n/home/hal/dev/interp/metis/experiments/blind-01/20261021-0116-loop/out/r1/card/RESULT.md:\n  Line 19: **Question.** Can computed capsid–receptor binding calls for ALPL, AAVR and LRP6 reproduce published bind/no-bind readouts with interfaces at the threefold axis?\n\n  Line 21: **What is known.** Shay and colleagues in 2024 ran five AlphaFold-Multimer models per peptide and used cryo-ET to map where LRP6 and IL3 bin